        <li>Roeder, Peter, and Karl Rich. "The Global Effort to Eradicate Rinderpest." IFPRI Discussion Paper 00923, November 2009. (The development of cell culture techniques and Plowright's passage of the virulent Kabete O virus through 70 passages in bovine kidney cells to produce a tissue culture vaccine that caused neither lesions nor fever and was safe for cattle of all breeds, ages and both sexes; the serum-simultaneous method, the short shelf life and the risk of other infections carried in the inoculum; the inactivated vaccines and the danger of waning immunity and silent spread; the goat-adapted Kabete vaccine and its use on some 15 million head of cattle between 1941 and 1948; India's National Project on Rinderpest Eradication from 1954 with its target of 320 million animals and 80 percent coverage, the eight-year campaign in Uttar Pradesh, and the 23 further years of escalating vaccination in Andhra Pradesh under which the incidence remained intractably high; the conclusion by the mid-1980s that the Indian program was failing and had become institutionalized; Joint Project 15 from 1961 and the Pan-African Rinderpest Campaign from 1986; the second great African rinderpest pandemic of the early 1980s, the losses in Nigeria, and the suicides among Fulani herders who lost their herds; the Thermovax thermostable vaccine developed by Mariner and colleagues in 1990 and the community-based vaccination programs in Sudan, Somalia, Kenya, Ethiopia, Tanzania, Uganda and Afghanistan; the reversion to virulence of the K37/70 vaccine and the rinderpest outbreaks in Georgia in 1989, Russia near the Mongolian border in 1991 and the Amur region in 1998, and the isolation from sick cattle in Kenya and Tanzania in the 1990s of a virus with the characteristics of the Kabete strain; the last cases in Pakistan near Karachi in October 2000; and the last detection near Meru National Park in Kenya in October 2001.)</li>
        <li>Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations. EMPRES Transboundary Animal Diseases Bulletin No. 11, "Rinderpest." 1999. (The presentation of the 1999 World Food Prize to Walter Plowright; the estimate that additional production in India alone from 1965 to 1998 amounted to 289 billion US dollars and corresponding estimates for Africa over the same period to 47 billion US dollars; the 1890s epidemic that killed 80 to 90 percent of all cattle in sub-Saharan Africa; the 1982 to 1984 outbreak estimated to have cost at least 500 million US dollars, and a single Nigerian outbreak in the 1980s responsible for losses of more than 2 billion US dollars; the FAO director general's 1957 statement that ten years earlier some two million cattle were dying in the Far East every year from rinderpest alone while the contemporary figure was probably less than 50,000; the first recognition of rinderpest as a distinct cattle plague in AD 376 to 386; the estimate that 200 million cattle were killed in Western Europe in the eighteenth century; and the founding of the first veterinary school in Lyons in 1762.)</li>
        <li>McVety, Amanda Kay. "The Second Disease Eradication: Rinderpest." Origins, Ohio State University, December 2025. (The invitation to Robert Koch from the Cape Colony in 1896 and his reasoning that prophylactic inoculation had been achieved against smallpox and rabies without knowing their microbes; his passaging of the virus through goats, pigs, antelopes, donkeys, mules, dogs, eagles, pigeons, chickens, rabbits, mice, guinea pigs and a secretary bird, the drying of infected blood, the use of chemicals and of serum and bile from recovered animals, and the absence of any clear success before he was ordered to join the expedition to India to study plague; the inactivated vaccines and their short immunity; the fact that rinderpest exists as a single serotype, so immunity to one strain protects against all; the creation of the OIE in 1924 after the 1920 Antwerp outbreak; the thermostable vaccine able to survive 30 days without refrigeration; the shift of power to local communities for vaccination and monitoring; the last detection in wild buffalo in Mount Meru National Park in Kenya in 2001 and the last vaccination in 2006; and the declaration of freedom in 2011.)</li>
        <li>Mariner, Jeffrey C., et al. "Rinderpest Eradication: Appropriate Technology and Social Innovations." Science 337, no. 6100 (2012): 1309-1312. (The development of a thermostable variant of the tissue culture rinderpest vaccine that can be used in the field for weeks without cold-chain support, and the community-based vaccination and participatory disease-searching methods used in remote areas.)</li>
        <li>Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations. EMPRES Transboundary Animal Diseases Bulletin No. 38. (The finding that the last evidence for rinderpest virus circulation dates to 2001 in the Somali ecosystem in Kenya.)</li>
